lesson reading-what-the-word-means v=1 format=teach grade=3 unit=2 subject=reading levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "What the word means here"
  goal "Work out what a word means from the sentence it sits in, and tell a saying from words that mean exactly what they say."

  look {
    scene 34x15 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        row top space=1 align=end {
          passage p title="Up the hill" text="At the top we found a spring and filled our bottles." width=18
          art hills asset="hills"
        }
        wordcards cards words=["spring", "slick", "steep"] picked=0
      }
    }
    say """
      A word can mean more than one thing, and the sentence around it decides which. Spring is a
      season, and it is also water coming up out of the ground. Nothing but the sentence tells you
      which one is meant.

      Some sentences do not mean exactly what their words say. Over the moon means very happy, not
      up in space.
      """
  }
  do {
    show reading.word-meaning k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level easy {
      worked reading.which-meaning k=0 s=2 level=medium
    }
    practice reading.which-meaning count=2 seed=6
    practice reading.which-sentence-uses count=2 seed=2
    practice reading.made-up-word count=2 hard-count=1 seed=3
    level hard {
      practice reading.literal-or-not count=1 seed=1
    }
    show reading.really-means k=0 easy-k=1 hard-k=3
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show reading.literal-or-not k=1 easy-k=2 hard-k=0
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show reading.invent-a-word k=0
  }
  remember {
    say "Read the whole sentence the word is in. The sentence picks the meaning."
  }

  grown-ups """
    When the child meets a word they do not know, read the sentence again without stopping at it
    and then ask what would make sense there. Guessing from context is the skill, not looking it up.
    The made-up words make the point: nobody knows them, so the sentence is the only clue. In the
    two-star question, ask of each sentence whether it could happen word for word. The three-star
    question turns it round: the child writes the clues for a word of their own.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the word in the passage is steep, a spring in the ground is worked once in the teacher's pen before the senses children use most (the season, a lamp, a game, an elephant's trunk, the rides), the made-up words are a zib and a flim, the saying is raining cats and dogs, the two-star's sentence is the same one, and the three-star is as written."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the word in the passage is slick, the senses are the rarer ones (a spring in the ground, a light blue coat, socks that match, a trunk in the attic, fair hair), one made-up word makes way for a second sentence that does not mean what it says, the made-up word is the grunkle, the saying is a piece of cake, the two-star's sentence is Kit over the moon, and the three-star is as written."
  }
}
